# Review package: batch 5, slice delta

## bccf989 Keep the name edge for the edge, stop browsers reusing Caddy for a live company, and say what the relay can read

Batch 5's one fix round on plan 1 Task 9, relay TLS routing.

Critical. A legacy login with run id edge was registered by frps under client id edge, so anyone with a binding's key
could keep the real edge from logging in again, and hornhoof.org, the API, the tunnels and every http-mode company went
with it. A reserved_run_id rule now refuses that run id for every login that is not the edge. Seen red on the real frps
wire with "client_id [edge] for user [] is already online".

Critical. All names share one address and Caddy holds a certificate for every one of them, so a browser reused its
HTTP/2 connection to Caddy for a company whose tunnel was up, and Caddy read that request and answered 404. Caddy now
speaks HTTP/1.1 only, a Host other than the connection's name gets 421 with strict_sni_host, and a 404 or 410 under
*.hornhoof.org ends its connection, so that a tab polling across a leader switch reaches the tunnel once it is back.

Important. The README and two comments said hornhoof.org sees only encrypted bytes for an https company. That holds
while its tunnel is up. With no tunnel the name falls back to Caddy, which reads that request to answer 404 or 410 and
keeps nothing. They say so now. The design choice stays with Ivan and David AI, QUESTIONS.md item 13.

Important. test_caddy_https_on_loopback_only proved the harness, because its global default_bind bound everything. Only
the test Caddy's http site is bound to loopback now, so that https rests on the Caddyfile's own lines. Seen failing in a
copy with each of the three binds removed, one at a time.

Minor: the https port's probe no longer delays health and is skipped while frps does not answer; a token with a lone
surrogate is refused; edge-frpc.service wants frps.service; the passthrough echo takes a picked port and close joins its
thread; the trace says edge for the edge; render_caddyfile's error names the right file.

Ruling: the https probe moved to a loop of its own, watch_passthrough, beyond the fix list's "stamp when frps answers" - why: a probe only moved behind the stamp still stands between two stamps, and the test stayed red with that reorder alone (0.6 s frps and a 1 s probe age the stamp to 2.1 s, past HEALTH_MAX_AGE_S) - cost if wrong: one more daemon thread, and health's passthrough can lag one loop behind while frps stays up
Ruling: Connection: close goes by status on all of *.hornhoof.org, so it also covers a 404 or 410 from an http-mode company's own app and from a company on the SSH tunnel - why: to Caddy frps's 404 and a company's own look the same - cost if wrong: such a company pays a new TLS handshake after each of its own 404s until it moves to https
Ruling: test_server_name_is_what_the_handshake_carries changes from 404 to 421, the one old test changed - why: the lead's ruling B says so - cost if wrong: none, the test follows the Caddyfile
Ruling: the harness's anchor for hornhoof.org's test certificate no longer holds the bind line - why: removing that bind must fail on the socket test and not on rendering - cost if wrong: none, only the harness

